  "id": "886029329",
  "subject": [
    "ANE"
  ],
  "title": "Step Gate of the Gardens",
  "provenance": "Pleiades",
  "placeTypeURIs": [
    "https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/place-types/city-gate"
  ],
  "details": "<p>The gate is mentioned in an Akkadian inscription of the Neo-Assyrian king Sennacherib dating to 691 B.C. and, therefore, must have been built sometime between mid-694 and early 691. In that text, the Step Gate of the Palace is the third gate of the western stretch of wall; the gates are listed from north to south.</p>\r\n<p><span>Igisigsig-mu\u0161ammeh-\u1e63ipp\u0101ti was originally the Akkadian ceremonial name of the Gate of the Garden, before it became the S\u00een Gate.</span></p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "Nineveh's western wall had seven (or eight) gates: the Step Gate of the Gardens was the third gate from the northern city wall. The Neo-Assyrian king Sennacherib constructed it sometime between 694 and 691 B.C. and he gave it the Akkadian ceremonial name Igisigsig-mu\u0161ammeh-\u1e63ipp\u0101ti, which means \"The God Igisigsig Is the One Who Makes Orchards Flourish.\" The gate is still unlocated, but, based on its everyday name Akkadian name mu\u0161l\u0101lum \u0161a kir\u00ee (\"Step Gate of the Gardens\"), it must have led up to Sennacherib's Palace on the citadel, perhaps at the foot of the sloping passage Room LI of the South-West Palace.",
